const addJapanPoliticsSection=(slug,beforeId,section)=>{const article=window.deepArticles[slug];if(!article||article.sections.some(existing=>existing.id===section.id))return;const index=article.sections.findIndex(existing=>existing.id===beforeId);if(index<0)article.sections.push(section);else article.sections.splice(index,0,section);};
addJapanPoliticsSection("politics-of-japan","limits",{id:"claim-chains",title:"Political claim chains",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-mnf55 writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:44:34-06:00 --><p>Political influence moved through several chains whose outputs were different. A resident, municipality, association, or company could supply records, petitions, votes, funds, expertise, employment, or a threat of withdrawal. Diet members and factions converted some of these inputs into questions, appropriations, committee work, appointments, and ministry access. Bureaucratic offices converted them into permits, standards, grants, inspections, credit decisions, and administrative schedules. Court and service institutions retained access to protected strategic questions.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Claimant or channel</th><th>Usable political instrument</th><th>Constitutional limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constituency, municipality, or prefecture</td><td>Election, petition, local budget, service record, land decision, and Diet brokerage</td><td>National grants, police supervision, standards, and strategic projects restricted refusal</td></tr><tr><td>Recognized faction or independent member</td><td>Supply vote, committee scrutiny, candidate endorsement, appointment bargain, and public criticism</td><td>The throne, supreme command, and imperial settlement remained protected conclusions</td></tr><tr><td>Ministry, court circle, or armed service</td><td>Expertise, regulation, appointment access, strategic warning, and implementation control</td><td>Ordinary policy still required money, law, administration, and accommodation with peer institutions</td></tr><tr><td>Corporate house, bank, or trade association</td><td>Campaign support, technical information, projects, employment, credit, and coordinated investment</td><td>A firm did not acquire lawful sovereignty or the Emperor's constitutional office</td></tr><tr><td>Associated government</td><td>Diplomacy, treaty implementation, national law, permits, taxation, and Economic Board bargaining</td><td>It did not become a Japanese Diet constituency and faced Japanese control of finance, standards, routes, and corporate capacity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>By 2000, dominant internal Japanese politics generally treated zaibatsu-led economic integration as the system working as intended. Corporate abuses appeared as scandals, failures of caution, or matters for correction rather than evidence of an unresolved constitutional crisis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Firms possessed no throne, nationality law, or formal right of supreme command, yet their control of infrastructure, careers, finance, information, and standards gave them vetoes with effects resembling public power.</p><p>Post-preservationist politicians defended this plural chain when it kept employment, transport, relief, taxation, and ordinary government operating. They preferred correction through budgets, inspection, management change, or negotiated terms to the reconstruction of earlier political communities. The test favored claimants already able to produce Japanese-language records, expert reports, campaign finance, and continuous services. Police exclusion, colonial schooling, land seizure, unequal revenue, and corporate control of information had helped determine who could meet it.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
addJapanPoliticsSection("political-parties-factions-postwar-japan","prohibitions",{id:"corporate-alignment",title:"Corporate power and factional alignment",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-jfctd writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:44:34-06:00 --><p>From the 1970s, disputes over corporate power produced three broad alignments inside Japanese politics.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> They operated across court, bureaucracy, services, companies, banks, journalism, and parliamentary factions rather than as three fixed canonical parties.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Alignment</th><th>Institutional base</th><th>Principal claim</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Guardianship establishment</td><td>Court circles, younger diplomats, developmental officials, and some military planners</td><td>Corporate monopolies could destroy associated-state trust and make Japanese political promises strategically unusable</td></tr><tr><td>Imperial commercial bloc</td><td>Corporate executives, banking interests, older continental administrators, and sympathetic politicians</td><td>Japanese capital, risk, infrastructure, and security entitled firms to retain direction of the systems they had built</td></tr><tr><td>National-populist critics</td><td>Smaller firms, consumers, journalists, and politicians hostile to corporate privilege inside Japan</td><td>Zaibatsu power distorted prices, suppressed domestic competitors, and placed overseas corporate costs upon the Japanese public</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>During the late 1980s and 1990s, Japanese firms increasingly used organized patronage and political sponsorship across the Sphere. By 2000, serious campaigns, infrastructure delivery, prestigious career placement, and economic growth in many associated states were difficult to sustain against the opposition of dominant Japanese corporate groups, and corporate sponsorship had become expected in much of the political field.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Inside Japan, funds, expertise, committee access, project location, employment, and administrative information strengthened sympathetic factions. Exact campaign-finance rules and sponsor disclosures remain open.</p><p>Post-preservationist factions usually distinguished corporate localization from surrender of strategic command. They could accept national managers, local shareholders, taxes, training, and public-service duties while retaining Japanese standards, capital networks, and final security interests. This framework gave associated politicians real bargaining subjects and permitted victories over particular contracts. It also made structural dependence appear to be ordinary coalition politics and encouraged Tokyo to describe resistance as patronage competition or administrative obstruction.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
addJapanPoliticsSection("political-opposition-postwar-japan","prohibited",{id:"remedy",title:"Complaint and remedy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-nxys6 writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:44:34-06:00 --><p>A lawful complaint normally identified an official act, administrative failure, corporate practice, or budgetary choice and placed evidence before a recognized venue. A petition could seek a road, school, price decision, investigation, or appointment. A legislator or newspaper could expose accounts and responsibility. A local assembly, professional body, company council, or university could document the effect upon its members. The available remedy might be a revised appropriation, new inspection, changed contract, disciplined official, altered route, local concession, or replacement of management. Exact procedural rights differed or remain open.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Lawful form</th><th>Protected boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Was a ministry, police office, governor, or contractor incompetent or corrupt?</td><td>Evidence, petition, press exposure, Diet scrutiny, audit, appointment pressure, or administrative correction</td><td>The criticism did not create a general right to organize against the throne or imperial order</td></tr><tr><td>Did a company abuse monopoly, housing, prices, employment, or political access?</td><td>Inspection, contract revision, tax or permit dispute, council grievance, public scandal, or ministerial discipline</td><td>A particular abuse could be corrected while corporate direction remained legitimate</td></tr><tr><td>Could a locality or region maintain a different service arrangement?</td><td>Budget, staffing, transport, relief, tax, and maintenance proposal through local and national institutions</td><td>Cultural distinctiveness alone did not transfer nationality or strategic authority</td></tr><tr><td>Could an associated state restrain a Japanese firm?</td><td>National legislation, permits, taxation, negotiation, courts, diplomacy, and common institutions</td><td>Its claim was interstate or treaty bargaining, not participation in Japanese opposition politics</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>By 2000, Japanese politicians usually interpreted a corporate scandal as misconduct within a generally successful system. The expected answer was greater care, compliance, supervision, or compensation rather than repudiation of zaibatsu-led integration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Opposition could win a case while leaving the institutional source of repeated cases intact.</p><p>Post-preservationist thought strengthened the demand that critics offer an authority able to maintain law, employment, relief, transport, records, and peace after the contested arrangement changed. Burmese preservationist failures supplied a recurring warning against political restoration unsupported by administration. This requirement could improve scrutiny of symbolic programs and unworkable jurisdictions. It also placed the burden of institutional proof upon people denied revenue, information, legal organization, or access to strategic assets, and allowed coercive continuity to present itself as the only practical remedy.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
addJapanPoliticsSection("political-opposition-postwar-japan","prohibited",{id:"effective-choice",title:"Consent and effective choice",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-b-20260816-e7j4n writer=b kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:47:59-06:00 --><p>The jurisdictional separation remains necessary. An associated state used its own law, permits, taxation, courts, diplomacy, and treaty machinery rather than participating as a constituency in the Japanese Diet. Its ministers were not Japanese opposition spokesmen, and criticism inside Japan could not substitute for authorization by the associated government or representation of its population.</p><p>Separate jurisdiction did not establish equal capacity to choose. Corporate control of campaign finance, newspapers, technical records, prestigious employment, credit, transport, communications, and common standards could determine which candidates appeared credible and which remedies could be implemented. A formally national decision might therefore be lawful while resting upon a political field shaped by the firm subject to that decision.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Element of a remedy</th><th>Formal question</th><th>Effective-capacity question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standing and jurisdiction</td><td>Could the claimant invoke national law, a permit, contract, court, or treaty procedure?</td><td>Could the claimant enter the venue without Japanese or corporate sponsorship?</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Could an authority request accounts, technical records, or testimony?</td><td>Did it possess independent staff, language, data, and access sufficient to test the company's record?</td></tr><tr><td>Interim authority</td><td>Could a license, payment, project, route, or appointment be suspended?</td><td>Could government preserve employment and essential service while the dispute proceeded?</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement</td><td>Could a court, ministry, police service, or board issue a binding decision?</td><td>Would Japanese authorities, banks, insurers, suppliers, and treaty-resident managers comply with the decision?</td></tr><tr><td>Replacement</td><td>Could the contract or management be terminated?</td><td>Was another operator, source of credit, technical cadre, route, or standard practically available?</td></tr><tr><td>Political consent</td><td>Had an elected or otherwise lawful national institution approved the settlement?</td><td>Could opponents campaign, organize, obtain information, and survive economically after refusing dominant corporate patrons?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A Japanese company acquired no lawful sovereignty merely by making refusal expensive. Its ability to withdraw credit, employment, expertise, or infrastructure nevertheless could operate as an effective veto when the associated government lacked a replacement. Conversely, an associated state that enforced tax, labor, access, disclosure, or management terms demonstrated a real exercise of authority even if it could not displace the entire corporate system.</p><p>Corporate influence should therefore be recorded at the stage where it acted: candidate selection, campaign support, evidence production, ministerial bargaining, adjudication, enforcement, or replacement capacity. A victory over one contract did not prove consent to the wider order. Continued dependence did not prove that every national institution was fictitious. The record must show who could make the decision, who supplied the means to carry it out, and which alternatives remained lawful and practicable.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
addJapanPoliticsRelated("political-opposition-postwar-japan",{href:"corporate-politics-geacps.html",kicker:"Associated-state capacity",label:"Corporate Politics in the GEACPS"});
addJapanPoliticsSection("emperor-in-postwar-japanese-politics","hirohito",{id:"threshold",title:"Threshold of imperial action",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-fd02t writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:59:42-06:00 --><p>The political importance of imperial action depended upon the question reaching the proper threshold. Ordinary administration remained with ministers, ministries, the Diet, courts, services, and local government. The throne became decisive when a Cabinet had to be formed across institutional vetoes, government and supreme command required one reserved settlement, or a major diplomatic compact needed court sponsorship beyond the authority of a single ministry.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Preparation before imperial action</th><th>Effect of imperial action</th><th>Execution afterward</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ordinary statute or budget</td><td>Cabinet proposal, ministry papers, Diet procedure, and fiscal settlement</td><td>Constitutional sanction and promulgation</td><td>Appropriation, regulation, administration, audit, and adjudication by competent bodies</td></tr><tr><td>Cabinet transition</td><td>Assessment of parliamentary support, service confidence, ministerial cooperation, court advice, and administrative capacity</td><td>Appointment and legitimation of a government able to operate</td><td>Ministers assume portfolios and secure law, supply, appointments, and implementation</td></tr><tr><td>Reserved strategic decision</td><td>Cabinet, Army, Navy, diplomatic, economic, intelligence, and command submissions; exceptional conference where required</td><td>Sanction of the common limit or course</td><td>Commands, diplomacy, mobilization, finance, and review remain with the responsible institutions</td></tr><tr><td>Sphere concord or associated-state settlement</td><td>Negotiation by Japanese and associated governments, treaty and strategic review, and identification of national implementing powers</td><td>Court sponsorship expresses Japanese commitment and imperial prestige</td><td>Each government uses its own law, permits, commands, budgets, and institutions</td></tr><tr><td>Regional cultural claim inside Japan</td><td>Evidence from local government, schools, archives, religious or community bodies, ministries, and affected households</td><td>Imperial patronage may recognize inheritance or concord</td><td>Custody, grants, curricula, ceremonies, and services remain administrative matters unless constitutional status is expressly changed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Hirohito's sanction of the 1941 Limited-War Directive fixed targets and required a new Imperial Conference before a theater commander widened a clash with American forces.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> In 1947 the court and senior statesmen preserved Tōjō's honor while transferring ordinary government to a Cabinet suited to demobilization and settlement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> These episodes show imperial authority setting a boundary and legitimating a transition. They do not supply a record of private intervention in every later policy.</p><p>The Emperor remained Emperor of Japan rather than sovereign of the Co-Prosperity Sphere.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Court sponsorship of an associated-state agreement could strengthen trust and demonstrate Japanese responsibility, while the associated ruler, Cabinet, legislature, courts, and administration retained the acts required by their own law. Post-preservationist advisers used the same distinction for cultural questions: imperial recognition could honor an archive, shrine, school, language, or regional identity without creating a restored government. This restraint also protected a constitutional settlement formed through conquest, annexation, police suppression, and unequal access to the court. Rare imperial action carried authority partly because many affected populations had no equal route to place their account before the sovereign.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
